From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: openworld-kernel <noreply@openworld>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drop_caches: skip redundant scans in balloon_drop_caches()

With 0003/0006, every stalled balloon allocation triggers a
system-wide cache drop (lru_add_drain_all + iterate_supers +
drop_slab) - up to 3x per batch, and then again on every
~200ms zero-progress retry of update_balloon_size (0010). Once
the balloon has taken everything reclaimable, each of those scans
finds nothing new and is pure CPU burn (a full superblock walk
plus a shrinker pass, repeated ~5x/second per stuck balloon).

Fingerprint the reclaimable set - file pages plus reclaimable
slab pages (the global node slab counters are stored in pages,
read warning-free via global_node_page_state_pages()) - and
skip the scan when it is unchanged since the last drop. New
caching moves the fingerprint, so anything actually droppable is
still picked up; a no-op retry takes the fast path instead.

Only the balloon call site changes; the user-facing
drop_caches sysctl handler still forces a drop.

Signed-off-by: openworld-kernel <noreply@openworld>


Signed-off-by: openworld-kernel <noreply@openworld>
---
 fs/drop_caches.c                | 33 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 0 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/drop_caches.c b/fs/drop_caches.c
index 123456789038..123456789039 100644
--- a/fs/drop_caches.c
+++ b/fs/drop_caches.c
@@ -56,13 +56,47 @@
  * balloon driver when inflation stalls on cached memory. Only clean data
  * is dropped; dirty pages are left for writeback as usual.
  */
+
+/*
+ * Fingerprint of the reclaimable set: file pages plus reclaimable slab
+ * pages (the global node slab counters are stored in pages). The
+ * balloon driver calls balloon_drop_caches() on every allocation stall
+ * (up to 3x per batch, then the ~5Hz zero-progress backoff), and each
+ * call is a system-wide scan (lru_add_drain_all + iterate_supers +
+ * drop_slab). Re-scanning when the reclaimable set is unchanged since
+ * the last drop is pure CPU burn: the previous drop already freed
+ * everything there was to free.
+ */
+static atomic64_t balloon_reclaimable_snapshot;
+
+static unsigned long balloon_reclaimable_fingerprint(void)
+{
+	return global_node_page_state(NR_FILE_PAGES) +
+	       global_node_page_state_pages(NR_SLAB_RECLAIMABLE_B);
+}
+
 void balloon_drop_caches(void)
 {
+	unsigned long snap;
+
+	snap = balloon_reclaimable_fingerprint();
+	if ((u64)atomic64_read(&balloon_reclaimable_snapshot) == snap)
+		return;
+	atomic64_set(&balloon_reclaimable_snapshot, snap);
+
 	lru_add_drain_all();
 	iterate_supers(drop_pagecache_sb, NULL);
 	count_vm_event(DROP_PAGECACHE);
 	drop_slab();
 	count_vm_event(DROP_SLAB);
+
+	/*
+	 * Record what is left after the drop: a retry whose fingerprint
+	 * is unchanged since this point has nothing new to reclaim, so
+	 * it takes the fast path above.
+	 */
+	atomic64_set(&balloon_reclaimable_snapshot,
+		     balloon_reclaimable_fingerprint());
 }
 
 static int drop_caches_sysctl_handler(const struct ctl_table *table, int write,
-- 
2.47.0
